  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 213 PM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nWe're watching a small low pressure system spin its way through\nnorthern Minnesota this afternoon, tracking approximately east-\nsoutheast. With this, there is a small dry slot bringing a brief\nperiod of sunshine to far northwest Wisconsin and northeast\nMinnesota while light snow is falling around and northwest of \nthe low center. The snow will continue moving southeast with the\nlow through this afternoon and evening. \n\nThe snow squall parameter is lighting up for this afternoon due\nto the combination of steep low-level lapse rates, gusty winds,\nand potential for snow showers. While air temperatures may start\nto fall below freezing later this afternoon as snow showers pass\nthrough, road temperatures are (at least currently) several\ndegrees above freezing per MNDOT observations. Webcams up in\nnorth-central Minnesota, where some snow has fallen, shows just\nwet roads. With that said, snow squalls are not expected in\nterms of anything that would warnings. We will see some snow \nshowers briefly reduce visibility below a mile, and combined \nwith gusty winds, this could lead to some localized hazardous \nconditions for travelers.\n\nThe radar is matching pretty well to what short-range CAMs are\nsuggesting as this low passes through this evening, which is a\nfairly tight convergence band of snow, which is going to be what\nwill have the highest potential to drop visibilities this\nafternoon and evening. After the low passes through, a bit of\nlingering moisture will likely produce some horizontal\nconvective roll snow showers, aided by the larger inland lakes\nlike Lake of the Woods. Some places in the Arrowhead could pick\nup close to an inch, but for most, we will be talking about\nflurries with no accumulation to a dusting or half an inch.\n\nOn the South Shore, as the low passes through, that convergent\nband of snow is expected to drop down across the South Shore \nand then switch over to lake-effect as colder air filters in.\nWind direction is expected to be almost due northwesterly, and\nwith that, the more intense lake-effect bands should target the\nUP. We may see just enough fetch and wind convergence such that\nparts of northeast Iron County, including the Hurley area, may\nsee some heavier snow showers tonight into Monday morning. A few\ninches of snow may fall there, and combined with gusty winds for\nthe Monday morning commute, there may be a bit of blowing snow\nand reduced visibilities. With this being one of the first snows\nof the season, and potential for Monday morning commute impacts,\nI issued a Winter Weather Advisory. While snowfall totals to 4\nor 5 inches are at the high end of possibilities, 2 or 3 inch\namounts are probable and the gusty winds will be icing on the\ncake to produce some impacts. \n\nSnow should be done everywhere Monday morning, just lingering\nthrough the morning for northern Iron County before it tapers\noff there too midday with dry air quickly filtering in from the\nnorthwest. High pressure will approach from the west, taking its\ntime to pass through on Tuesday. Expect quiet weather as a\nresult. \n\nThe jet stream pattern aloft will feature split flow and, in\ngeneral, disorganization. We may get a shortwave trough passing\nthrough going into Wednesday, but without much moisture to work\nwith, we may just end up with more clouds. Thursday is looking\npretty sunny with some drier air passing through, then maybe\nsome more clouds Friday. Our weather will be relatively boring\ncompared to a nor'easter that's looking to impact the east coast\nThursday into Friday. \n\nAn atmospheric river will impact Washington/Oregon around the\nsame time, and we might get some residual moisture from that\nspilling into our region for the weekend. We may get a low\npressure system moving in from the west that could bring some\nperiods of rain and snow. As of now, nothing appears significant\nin terms of rain or snow, but it may be a bit more of an active\nweather pattern in general. Precipitation type may depend a lot\non what time of day precipitation is passing through, as we may\nbe warm enough to be above freezing during the day.",
